  "nameStory": "Shrubby St. John's-wort gets its name from its woody, shrub-like growth habit that sets it apart from its more herbaceous cousins. The species name 'prolificum' means 'prolific' in Latin, referring to its abundant stamens that create showy, bristly flower centers. Like other St. John's-worts, it's named after St. John the Baptist, as many species traditionally bloom around St. John's Day (June 24th).",
  "description": "Shrubby St. John's-wort is a compact deciduous shrub that typically grows 2-4 feet tall and wide. It produces clusters of bright yellow, five-petaled flowers with conspicuous centers packed with numerous long stamens that give the blooms a fuzzy, bottlebrush appearance. The small, narrow leaves are arranged opposite each other along the stems, and the plant has a dense, rounded growth habit. Unlike many of its herbaceous relatives, this species develops woody stems that persist year-round.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Bright yellow flowers with 5 rounded petals and a spectacular center of numerous long, thread-like stamens that create a bristly appearance - count 50+ stamens per flower if you look closely",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Woody shrub structure, usually 2-4 feet tall with multiple branching stems that feel firm and rigid rather than soft and herbaceous",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Small, narrow oval leaves arranged in opposite pairs along the stem, with a smooth texture and slightly waxy feel when rubbed between fingers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Small brown capsules that split open to release tiny seeds, often persisting into winter and making a subtle rattling sound when shaken",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bark",
      "description": "Older stems develop thin, peeling bark with a reddish-brown color underneath, while young twigs remain smooth and green",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Shrubby St. John's-wort prefers well-drained, sandy or rocky soils in full sun to partial shade. Look for it along roadsides, in old fields, rocky slopes, and woodland edges throughout New England. It's particularly fond of disturbed sites and can tolerate poor, dry soils where many other shrubs struggle.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New leaves emerge as small, bright green opposite pairs, and flower buds begin forming at branch tips in late spring.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming occurs from June through August, with the most spectacular display of yellow, stamens-heavy flowers typically in July.",
    "fall": "Leaves may turn yellow before dropping, and brown seed capsules develop and ripen, often persisting on the branches.",
    "winter": "Bare woody stems show the shrub's branching pattern, with persistent brown seed capsules and reddish-brown bark visible on older stems."
  },
  "funFact": "Each flower contains 50-75 individual stamens - those thread-like structures in the center - and if you gently brush against a fresh bloom, your finger will come away dusted with bright yellow pollen that was once used as a natural dye!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other St. John's-worts, but shrubby St. John's-wort is the only one in New England that forms a true woody shrub. Memory trick: if it has yellow flowers with lots of stamens but you can't bend the stems easily, you've found the 'shrubby' one!",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Many St. John's-worts have been used medicinally throughout history, though shrubby St. John's-wort is less well-known for this than its famous cousin, common St. John's-wort (Hypericum perforatum). The name 'wort' is an old English word simply meaning 'plant' or 'herb.'",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's St. John's-worts, shrubby St. John's-wort stands out for its woody stems - most relatives like common St. John's-wort and spotted St. John's-wort are herbaceous perennials that die back to the ground each winter. Bushy St. John's-wort is also woody but has much smaller, more numerous flowers, while shrubby St. John's-wort produces fewer but larger, more showy blooms. The prolific stamens are more noticeable in shrubby St. John's-wort than in most of its relatives.",
